A metaclass `__new__(mcs, name, bases, namespace)` receives the metaclass itself, the new class's name, a tuple of its base classes and a dict of everything defined in the class body. It returns the new class, usually by calling `super().__new__`.

```python
class Meta(type):
    def __new__(mcs, name, bases, namespace):
        namespace["created_by"] = "Meta"
        return super().__new__(mcs, name, bases, namespace)

class Thing(metaclass=Meta):
    x = 1

print(Thing.created_by, Thing.x)   # Meta 1
```

