A red-black tree is a self-balancing binary search tree. Its rules are: every node is red or black; the root is black; every leaf (None) counts as black; a red node never has a red child; and every path from a node to its leaves has the same number of black nodes. Together these keep the longest path at most twice the shortest, so search, insert and delete are all O(log n). Insertions and deletions restore the rules with recolouring and rotations.

```python
def valid(node):  # returns black-height, or -1 if a rule is broken
    if node is None:
        return 1
    l, r = valid(node.left), valid(node.right)
    if -1 in (l, r) or l != r:
        return -1
    if node.red and ((node.left and node.left.red) or (node.right and node.right.red)):
        return -1
    return l + (0 if node.red else 1)
```

