Given matrices with dimensions `dims[i-1] x dims[i]`, find the parenthesization with the fewest scalar multiplications. The number of ways grows like the Catalan numbers, so brute force is hopeless. Dynamic programming over chain lengths solves it in O(n^3): `cost[i][j] = min over k of cost[i][k] + cost[k+1][j] + dims[i-1]*dims[k]*dims[j]`.

```python
def matrix_chain(dims):
    n = len(dims) - 1
    cost = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]
    for length in range(2, n + 1):
        for i in range(n - length + 1):
            j = i + length - 1
            cost[i][j] = min(
                cost[i][k] + cost[k + 1][j] + dims[i] * dims[k + 1] * dims[j + 1]
                for k in range(i, j)
            )
    return cost[0][n - 1]

print(matrix_chain([10, 30, 5, 60]))  # 4500
```

Here (A x B) x C costs 4500, versus 27000 for A x (B x C).

