Interpolation search is a binary search variant for sorted, roughly uniformly distributed numbers. Instead of the middle, it guesses the position by linear interpolation between the endpoints, like opening a phone book near the right letter. It averages O(log log n) on uniform data but degrades to O(n) on skewed data.

```python
def interpolation_search(a, x):
    lo, hi = 0, len(a) - 1
    while lo <= hi and a[lo] <= x <= a[hi]:
        if a[hi] == a[lo]:
            return lo if a[lo] == x else -1
        pos = lo + (x - a[lo]) * (hi - lo) // (a[hi] - a[lo])
        if a[pos] == x:
            return pos
        if a[pos] < x:
            lo = pos + 1
        else:
            hi = pos - 1
    return -1

data = list(range(0, 1000, 10))
print(interpolation_search(data, 370))  # 37
print(interpolation_search(data, 55))   # -1
```

Use plain binary search (`bisect`) when the distribution is unknown.

