The best case is O(n), when the list is already sorted. This needs an early-exit flag: if a full pass makes no swaps, the list is sorted and you stop. Without the flag, bubble sort is always O(n^2). Average and worst cases are O(n^2).

```python
def bubble(a):
    a = a[:]
    for end in range(len(a) - 1, 0, -1):
        swapped = False
        for i in range(end):
            if a[i] > a[i + 1]:
                a[i], a[i + 1] = a[i + 1], a[i]
                swapped = True
        if not swapped:
            break  # sorted: only one pass was needed
    return a

print(bubble([1, 2, 3, 4]))  # one pass, [1, 2, 3, 4]
print(bubble([3, 1, 2]))     # [1, 2, 3]
```

